-### Subtopics
-*   **SWAG-A (No Transient Detection):** Uses appearance modeling but no transient handling. Results in closer colors to ground truth than 3DGS but still suffers from floaters caused by transient objects [S1].
-*   **SWAG-T (No Appearance):** Uses transient detection but no appearance conditioning. Eliminates floaters and produces occluder-free scenes but suffers from color alterations (averaging) like base 3DGS [S1].
+### Parameters and Typical Values
+*   **Iterations:** 60,000 [S1].
+*   **Densification Interval:** 2,000 [S1].
+*   **Densification End:** 30,000 [S1].
+*   **Image Embedding Dimension:** 24 [S1].
+*   **Learning Rate (Image Embeddings):** 0.0025 [S1].
+*   **Hash Grid Size ($T$):** $2^{19}$ [S1].
+*   **Hash Grid Finest Resolution ($N_{\max}$):** 2048 [S1].
+*   **Hash Grid Coarsest Resolution ($N_{\min}$):** 16 [S1].
+*   **Hash Grid Levels ($L$):** 12 [S1].
+*   **Learning Rate (Hash Grid):** 0.0025 [S1].
+*   **MLP Layers:** 8 [S1].
+*   **MLP Hidden Size:** 51 [S1].
+*   **MLP Learning Rate:** 0.0025 [S1].
+
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Transient Object Holes:** In areas with frequent transient objects (e.g., clouds, tourists), the method may detect static parts as transient. Eliminating all transient Gaussians (those with high variance in $\tilde{\Delta \alpha}_I$) can leave black spots/holes in the scene [S1].
+*   **Mitigation:** To solve holes, Gaussians can be removed based on an adaptive threshold $\lambda$ rather than requiring zero variance ($\text{Var}(\tilde{\Delta \alpha}_I) < \lambda$). This requires manual tuning of $\lambda$ [S1].
+*   **Transient Percentage:** On average, less than 20% of Gaussians capture transient objects, ensuring the static scene is reconstructed without the blurry elongated Gaussians typical of view-dependent 3DGS [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training time almost doubles compared to base 3DGS [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Increased due to Hash Grid, MLP, and per-image embeddings. Hash Grid size is $2^{19}$ entries [S1].
-*   **Inference Time:** Inference time per frame is 10 times longer than 3DGS due to the MLP evaluation and Hash Grid lookups [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Handles unstructured in-the-wild data; disentangles static and dynamic Gaussians; enables controllable appearance via embedding interpolation; removes transient objects without supervision [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Slower training and inference than 3DGS; requires manual tuning of threshold $\lambda$ for transient removal to avoid holes; less than 20% of Gaussians capture transient objects on average [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When reconstructing scenes from unstructured internet photo collections with significant lighting variations and transient occluders (e.g., tourists, clouds) [S1].
-*   **When not to use:** When real-time performance is critical and the scene is static/controlled; when manual tuning of transient removal thresholds is undesirable [S1].
+*   **Training Time:** Almost doubles compared to standard 3DGS [S1]. However, it is orders of magnitude faster than previous in-the-wild NeRF-based baselines [S1].
+*   **Inference Time:** 10 times longer per frame than standard 3DGS due to the MLP and Hash Grid lookups [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Increased memory usage due to the storage of Hash Grids, Image Embeddings, and MLP weights [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Handles unstructured in-the-wild data; disentangles static and dynamic elements; enables controllable appearance via interpolation of image embeddings [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Slower inference than 3DGS; requires manual tuning of threshold $\lambda$ for transient removal to avoid holes; sensitive to the percentage of transient Gaussians [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** When reconstructing scenes from unstructured internet photo collections with significant lighting changes and transient occluders [S1].
+*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time performance comparable to standard 3DGS is required, or when the scene is static and well-structured [S1].
 
 ## Background
-SWAG was proposed by H. Dahmani et al. in the paper "SWAG: Splatting in the Wild images with Appearance-conditioned Gaussians" [S1]. It addresses the poor performance of standard 3DGS on unstructured in-the-wild data by incorporating appearance modeling and transient object handling [S1]. The method was evaluated on the Phototourism Dataset (Brandenburg Gate, Sacré Cœur, Trevi Fountain) and the NeRF-OSR Dataset [S1].
+SWAG was proposed by H. Dahmani et al. in 2024 [S1]. It builds upon the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [S1]. The method was evaluated on the Phototourism Dataset (Brandenburg Gate, Sacré Cœur, Trevi Fountain) and the NeRF-OSR Dataset [S1]. It addresses the limitations of 3DGS in unstructured scenarios, where view-dependent SH coefficients often lead to blurry artifacts and floaters caused by transient objects [S1].
 
 ## Sources
